      "text_raw": "It is not always easy to\nmodel the coupling between\nthe analog and digital\nblocks. A network of resis-\ntors and capacitors can be\nused, as shown in this slide.\nHowever it is a non-trivial\ntask to obtain realistic\nvalues of all these com-\nponents.\nBoth the analog and the\ndigital blocks are coupled\nby means of capacitors to a\ncommon substrate, which\ncan be modeled as a grid of\nresistors and capacitors.\nBoth the analog and the digital blocks have separate pins for supply lines and ground. They all\nconsist of inductors in series with small resistors.\nThe external supply line and ground are on top and at the bottom.\nThis applies to analog and digital blocks which are sufficiently small such that they do not\nhave to be subdivided over more blocks. If this is the case, then the simulation time grows\nconsiderably.",
      "text": "It is not always easy to model the coupling between the analog and digital blocks. A network of resistors and capacitors can be used, as shown in this slide. However it is a non-trivial task to obtain realistic values of all these components. Both the analog and the digital blocks are coupled by means of capacitors to a common substrate, which can be modeled as a grid of resistors and capacitors. Both the analog and the digital blocks have separate pins for supply lines and ground. They all consist of inductors in series with small resistors. The external supply line and ground are on top and at the bottom. This applies to analog and digital blocks which are sufficiently small such that they do not have to be subdivided over more blocks. If this is the case, then the simulation time grows considerably."
    }
  ],
  "extraction_status": "source_extracted",
  "semantic_review_status": "unreviewed",
  "commentary_text": "It is not always easy to model the coupling between the analog and digital blocks. A network of resistors and capacitors can be used, as shown in this slide. However it is a non-trivial task to obtain realistic values of all these components. Both the analog and the digital blocks are coupled by means of capacitors to a common substrate, which can be modeled as a grid of resistors and capacitors. Both the analog and the digital blocks have separate pins for supply lines and ground. They all consist of inductors in series with small resistors. The external supply line and ground are on top and at the bottom. This applies to analog and digital blocks which are sufficiently small such that they do not have to be subdivided over more blocks. If this is the case, then the simulation time grows considerably.",
